From b6f80914a872cf16c0efa85a7e1b01f9d27f1fe9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Tue, 7 Apr 2026 22:27:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] sepolicy: allow system_server to drive sysfs_leds backlight
 directly
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

DisplayPowerController on AOSP has a fallback path that reads
max_brightness and writes brightness directly under /sys/class/leds
when no light HAL responds. on the Blackview Shark 8 (MT6789) this
fallback is what actually drives the panel because the vendor light
HAL is not present in the GSI build. core sepolicy does not grant
system_server access to sysfs_leds, so the read on max_brightness
fails before the integer value can be computed and the brightness
write never happens. the framework dutifully ramps brightness from
0.17 down to 0 in DisplayManagerService but the panel sees nothing
and stays dark — the symptom users report as 'screen is black after
boot'.

allow system_server to search sysfs_leds dirs and read+write
sysfs_leds files. the lcd-backlight node is already labelled
sysfs_leds via the existing genfs_contexts entry; this rule
completes the chain so the fallback backlight path actually works.
---
 sepolicy/system_server.te | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 sepolicy/system_server.te

diff --git a/sepolicy/system_server.te b/sepolicy/system_server.te
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e106bf7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sepolicy/system_server.te
@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@
+# allow system_server (DisplayManagerService default backlight path) to read
+# max_brightness and write brightness on /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight when
+# no proper light HAL is available. without this, brightness ramps computed
+# in DisplayPowerController never reach the panel and the screen stays dark.
+allow system_server sysfs_leds:dir search;
+allow system_server sysfs_leds:file rw_file_perms;
